## Analysis

- **Generated Go parsers take {{ratioRange "Parse/{w}/generated/codepoints" "Parse/{w}/closure/codepoints" $all}}× the time of the closure backend**, and allocate about as much. With `-types`, `ParseAST` takes {{ratioRange "Parse/{w}/generated_ast" "Parse/{w}/generated/codepoints" $typed}}× the time of the generated `Parse` and a fraction of its memory, since it builds the grammar's Go types instead of a tree.
- **The bytecode VMs take {{ratioRange "Parse/{w}/bytecode/codepoints" "Parse/{w}/closure/codepoints" $all}}× (recursive) and {{ratioRange "Parse/{w}/iterative/codepoints" "Parse/{w}/closure/codepoints" $all}}× (iterative) the time of the closure backend.** The iterative VM pays for running rule calls and Pratt loops as state machines on its own stack.
- **The standard library is {{speedupRange "Parse/{w}/generated/codepoints" "Parse/{w}/{s}" $std}} times as fast as generated parsers** ({{speedupRange "Parse/{w}/closure/codepoints" "Parse/{w}/{s}" $std}} times as fast as the closure backend). The comparison is not like for like: `encoding/json`, `encoding/csv` and `encoding/xml` build no positioned typed tree, and `go/parser` uses a hand-written lexer. With typed values, JSON `ParseAST` takes {{factor "Parse/JSON/generated_ast" "Parse/JSON/encoding_json"}}× the time of `encoding/json`.
- **Recognition** is {{speedupRange "Parse/{w}/closure/codepoints" "Recognize/{w}/closure" $all}} times as fast as a full parse on the closure backend. It is still far from `json.Valid`, a hand-written state machine.
- **The position unit makes little difference**: byte positions take {{ratioRange "Parse/{w}/closure/bytes" "Parse/{w}/closure/codepoints" $all}}× the time of code points on the closure backend.
- **Incremental parsing** of minilang is {{speedupRange "Incremental/{w}/fresh" "Incremental/{w}/document" $backends}} times as fast as a full parse after a one-character edit. On the 50,000-record CSV document, an edit and a reparse take {{t "IncrementalLong/closure"}} on the closure backend: the reparse resumes the long repetition of records around the edit.
- **Streaming** runs at {{mbsRatio "Stream/closure" "Parse/CSV/closure/codepoints"}} times the throughput of batch parsing on the closure backend, while holding only its working set (memory does not grow with the input).
- **Loading `.pegoc`** takes {{ratioRange "Prepare/{w}/pegoc/closure" "Prepare/{w}/source" (list "json" "minilang")}}× (closure) and {{ratioRange "Prepare/{w}/pegoc-noast/bytecode" "Prepare/{w}/source" (list "json" "minilang")}}× (without the AST) the time of compiling from source.

## Default backend

The default backend (`pego.DefaultBackend`) is the **closure backend** when the grammar AST is available, and the **recursive bytecode VM** when it is not (a `.pegoc` file saved without the AST). The reasons are:

- Within a Go process, closures run the grammar with less overhead than bytecode: the recursive VM takes {{ratioRange "Parse/{w}/bytecode/codepoints" "Parse/{w}/closure/codepoints" $all}}× the time of the closure backend.
- Bytecode is a portable format; there is no reason to prefer it inside Go. A file without the AST cannot be turned into closures, so bytecode is used for it.
- The iterative VM is selected explicitly when deeply nested input must be parsed independently of the Go stack size.
- Generated Go parsers are compiled with the rest of the program and need no backend at run time; use them when the grammar is fixed.
